From: accel-virt
Subject: [PATCH] Rasterizer-ordered views are coherent between the fragments they order

A rasterizer-ordered UAV in a pixel shader got the invocation scope, so under
the Vulkan memory model its image reads and writes were private and the
interlock's barriers did not make one fragment's write visible to the next
fragment of the same pixel. Blends through an ROV came out in a different order
from run to run. A rasterizer-ordered UAV now gets the queue family scope, the
same as a globally coherent one, so its accesses are non-private texels.
---
diff --git a/spirv/spirv_builder.cpp b/spirv/spirv_builder.cpp
index 020430d..61d9b56 100644
--- a/spirv/spirv_builder.cpp
+++ b/spirv/spirv_builder.cpp
@@ -4683,7 +4683,7 @@ spv::Scope SpirvBuilder::getUavCoherentScope(ir::UavFlags flags) {
   if (flags & (ir::UavFlag::eReadOnly | ir::UavFlag::eWriteOnly))
     return spv::ScopeInvocation;
 
-  if (flags & ir::UavFlag::eCoherent)
+  if (flags & (ir::UavFlag::eCoherent | ir::UavFlag::eRasterizerOrdered))
     return spv::ScopeQueueFamily;
 
   if (m_stage == ir::ShaderStage::eCompute)
